WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.450
It's late. A script posting invoices to Orderful gets a four oh one. A teammate says: the key is dead, ask for a new one.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:17.050
But the script sent the wrong header. Orderful expects one called orderful A P I key. One line fixed, no embarrassing email.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:21.876
An A P I lets one program ask another program to do something.

00:00:21.876 --> 00:00:29.951
Think of a restaurant. You don't walk into the kitchen. You give the waiter an order in a set format, and get back your food, or a reason.

00:00:29.951 --> 00:00:36.551
Every request has four parts. The method says what you want to do. The U R L says which thing you mean.

00:00:36.551 --> 00:00:42.701
The headers are labels, like who you are. And the body is the content you send, usually Jason.

00:00:42.701 --> 00:00:49.151
The main methods: GET reads. POST creates. PUT and PATCH change. DELETE removes.

00:00:49.151 --> 00:00:54.526
GET is safe to repeat. POST is not. Send it twice, and you may get two records.

00:00:54.526 --> 00:01:02.976
The answer has a status code. Two hundreds mean it worked. Four hundreds mean fix your request. Five hundreds mean their side failed.

00:01:02.976 --> 00:01:07.976
Four oh one: they don't know who you are. Four oh three: they know you, but you're not allowed.

00:01:07.976 --> 00:01:12.776
For a four oh one, check the header name first, then the key, then the environment.

00:01:12.776 --> 00:01:18.851
Now, auth. An A P I key is like a building badge: one long secret, sent with every call.

00:01:18.851 --> 00:01:26.726
O auth trades credentials for a token. NetSuite's token based auth signs each request with secrets that never travel.

00:01:26.726 --> 00:01:34.101
Next, duplicates. Networks fail halfway. You post an order, the connection drops, and you never see the answer.

00:01:34.101 --> 00:01:41.751
If you just retry, you may create it twice. So check before you create: search by the P O or invoice number first.

00:01:41.751 --> 00:01:49.401
Another trap: A P Is return one page at a time. Read only page one, and you may miss the problems on page two.

00:01:49.401 --> 00:01:58.676
Polling versus webhooks. With polling, your system asks every few minutes: anything new? With a webhook, the other system calls you right away.

00:01:58.676 --> 00:02:09.126
A real lesson: a poller must approve each document after processing it. If it never does, the oldest item blocks the queue, and everything behind it looks missing.

00:02:09.126 --> 00:02:16.201
To test safely: read first, know your environment, and keep keys out of chats. Then send one write at a time.

00:02:16.201 --> 00:02:23.026
Remember: method, U R L, headers, body, and a status code back. Reads are free. Writes need care.

00:02:23.026 --> 00:02:27.876
Next, we look inside the body: Jason, Mosaic, and Jason-ah-tah.

